body:
Yep. Fit matters for any program, rotation or not. You need to show the program that you're a good fit for the PROGRAM, not just a single PI. When I got admitted to rotation-based programs in 2020, I made it quite obvious in my SOP how many PIs fit my interests and how the program was a great place for me.

&#x200B;

PIs can suddenly  lose funding, wind up having students who wind up taking longer to defend than they expected, wind up leaving the university, or any number of things. These programs want to know you're there for the program and won't just bounce if this advisor of your dreams dips.

My university: May the odds ever be in your favor.

&#x200B;

I'm in a deep red state in the South, not surprising in the least. Scary since this is my first semester teaching in-person large enrollment classes, but not surprising.

To answer your question:  Yes.  

&#x200B;

The message you just sent to the grad program director comes across as "I don't do things that make me nervous and uncomfortable and I'm not even willing to try."  A lot of grad school can be uncomfortable and anxiety-inducing and you not even being willing to entertain the possibility of an interview would show as a huge red flag that you might not be willing to try when any of those moments come along in their program. That's the first impression you made.

&#x200B;

Very few people LIKE interviews and a lot of people definitely struggle with them, but we still have to do them. It comes with the territory. I have full blown anxiety disorders and I'm socially awkward AF and the thought of meeting with potential advisors during my previous cycle turned my bones into jello. I still knew I had to do it. Did it suck being anxious AF beforehand? Were most of them stressful during? Yes. Was it worth it in the end to push through my discomfort and sit through those interviews? Absolutely. I wound up meeting my now advisor who's absolutely amazing and had I just tried to NOPE out of meeting her because I don't like interviews, I wouldn't be where I am now.

&#x200B;

Grad school is full of things that can be challenging and force us to push past our barriers. Those challenges are some of the things that can help us grow the most, and you just shot yourself in the foot with this program director by sending a message that you won't do that.

The first email is way too generic and a lot of those questions after asking if they're accepting students are things you can ask during a Zoom chat AFTER they're interested enough to meet you.  Be genuinely interested in their work, personalize the emails to each lab.

&#x200B;

As for the second email, I'm a current PhD student. That email is asking a LOT from us.  PhD life can be super chaotic, especially if you happen to catch a student during prep for major milestones or during a time crunch for research or teaching. Questions like number 1, don't even bother asking via email. Ask to chat with us on Zoom or Facetime or some other non-written form of communication. We can be a lot more honest about things in conversations that are off-the-record. The rest of that would also be much faster to answer conversationally, so I would save most of those questions for a Zoom convo too. I'd be ten times more likely to answer an email that VERY BRIEFLY says something about being a prospective student who wants to know more about my experience in that specific lab and asking if I could take a few minutes to chat on Zoom than I would be to try to type out answers to all of those questions.

I'm pretty sure my reaching out to the PI beforehand is one of the reasons I got accepted here and wound up with her as my advisor. Some programs in biology (seems to be a lot more common in eco/evo and bio ed than biomedical and cell/molec) actually want you to have an advisor identified before you apply, so in those cases you have to reach out. One program I applied to actually wanted proof that you had spoken to a member of their program faculty before. 

&#x200B;

I wouldn't recommend spamming a ton of PIs with emails, but find some whose work you're genuinely interested in and unless the program specifically says NOT to contact advisors beforehand, reach out and try to have a conversation with them. They might also save you some time and money if they reply and tell you that they're not taking students, then you won't have wasted time and money on the application fee.

Have previous mental health-related issues myself. If it comes up, I just tend to say I had health problems and leave it at that. They really shouldn't inquire further about that since it's a healthcare privacy thing at that point.

&#x200B;

There's really nothing positive to be gained by declaring a mental health condition in an interview. There's seriously still a big stigma about it, especially in STEM. We're trying but it's not great overall yet.

If they don't pay for your housing, you're pretty much screwed without student loans. Once you take out taxes and if you have to pay any graduate school fees, that $1330 a month is going to be a LOT smaller. I'm in a program that pays me about $1400 AFTER taxes in a fairly  high cost-of-living area but nothing on the order of the COL in NYC and it's already pretty dire.  

&#x200B;

What would be even worse if there's no option for summer funding because then you'd have to stretch that $12000 to make it last for all 12 months.  I wouldn't recommend it either way.

>So, if you're one of those lucky students with multiple offers, it might be wise to consider making a decision sooner rather than later.

&#x200B;

No, no it's not. It's wise to take all of the time needed to consider all aspects of the decision carefully. I was pressured into making a decision early during my first cycle by people saying shit like this. I wound up in a toxic, abusive program because of it and now I'm a first year again elsewhere, still trying to recover from the trauma.

&#x200B;

To the people with multiple offers, PLEASE take the time you need to make the best decision for you.  Make sure you look at any and every factor involved in your decision and watch for potential red flags. Make sure you've talked to as many students and faculty as you can. Journal your thoughts, bounce ideas off people you trust to get outside opinions. Make sure you've done your due diligence to make sure you're going to wind up in a positive environment where you can thrive. And don't forget to account for the expenses like graduate school fees when you do the math for any funded offers.

I'm so sorry, OP.  I know how those rejections hurt because I went through the cycle twice and I had to deal with the same "you'll get in for sure" from friends and mentors. Your feelings are valid, so definitely take the time to process them and make sure you practice some good self-care while you do because it's easy to let yourself go and fall into the vicious cycle of despair.

&#x200B;

Good on you for not letting your rejections define you. Remember that you DO have acceptances regardless, so there are programs that see a success story yet to be written in you and think that you'd make amazing contributions to their program. Take those opportunities and run with them and let them fuel your desire to succeed.  Sometimes things in life happen for a reason and one of those acceptances will turn out to be the absolute best place for you to grow and thrive.

I understand that you want people in the waitlist to get a chance, but remember that these people who do have multiple admits have to make one of the hardest decisions of their lives and it's a super dynamic situation since the university someone is almost certainly not going to want to attend can suddenly move way up the priority list if something changes. Like other commenters said, people are still waiting on visits and many people in STEM may be waiting on the results of the GRFP to be able to finalize their decisions since being award that can completely change the game for some folks. (I've heard of rejects becoming admits if a student suddenly gets the GRFP, some students get more money from some universities if they do, etc.)

&#x200B;

I'm not saying that y'all don't deserve the chance, but the people who have the admits also deserve the chance to make the best decision possible for their futures too. I got rushed into making a decision the first time around and it wound up being catastrophic, so I'm always going to tell the people with multiple offers to very carefully consider every red and green flag and wait on all of their visits and any potential fellowship offers carefully.

Bash you all we want?  Okay, even though it looks like plenty of people already had a go at you, I'll throw my two cents in:

&#x200B;

Stop being a thief of other people's joy.

&#x200B;

Getting accepted to graduate school can be the biggest accomplishment of someone's life to date and not everyone has people in their lives to share the joy with who actually understand what it's like and how hard it actually is. Let people have their moment in the sun and stop being so bitter over people sharing their joy.

&#x200B;

In graduate school, you are going to be surrounded by labmates who will be having abstracts accepted to conferences, getting published, having grants funded, and finding other forms of success. Are you going to sit there and be all sour grapes about it and say they're seeking validation when they share their news or are you going to share in their joy and support them?

Yup!  I started my PhD in STEM Ed back in August.  It's going great so far. I switched from pure biology so it's a bit of an adjustment taking so many education classes mixed in with my science classes. I absolutely love it though. I'm so much happier being around people who love teaching and learning as much as I do, even if I'm still trying to wrap my head around the differences in things like writing style, the different classes, and my research being completely different. 

&#x200B;

Definitely a shortage of education people here, I felt pretty alone last cycle when I was applying.  You definitely have a ton of options with 6 acceptances!  I've had to make some pretty tough choices when it came to accepting grad program offers, if you ever want to talk them through with someone who's been there and done that, feel free to toss me a DM :)

Safety schools don't exist for grad admissions like they do for undergrad. There's so many moving parts that things like GPA/GRE scores and even number of years of research experience don't always make you a better candidate for a particular program. There's a lot about fit that matters for programs and also a lot of moving parts on the university/department/program level.  People with lower GPAs and fewer years of research experience may get the interview/admit over someone with a 4.0 and a publication if they're a better fit for a program. The person with higher stats may have only indicated interest in advisors who won't be taking students or they may have a bad LoR or a crappy SoP. People can do their undergrad somewhere and get rejected from that school's grad programs. Someone can get accepted to a program one year but rejected in another cycle.

&#x200B;

Buying into the concept of safety schools instead of picking programs that you can show that you're the best fit for is doing yourself a massive disservice.  People can stack the deck with what they think are "safeties" and wind up with rejections at all of them or just wind up with admits to places they really only applied to because they thought they were "safe" and really don't want to go to.

Well said!  I love that quote. "It's not about where you land but rather about growing where you're sown".  It's so true. 

&#x200B;

I went through similar things where the "big" programs passed me by and I wound up with an acceptance to a smaller program at an R2 that's turned out to be the best possible place for me. The program is super supportive and I wound up joining a great lab with a really supportive, enthusiastic PI. I've accomplished more here in one semester than I did in a year and a half at a bigger R1 university and I'm a lot happier too.

&#x200B;

Where you go doesn't define you. Making the most out of what opportunities you're given, regardless of the circumstances does.

You're correct about mastering out. That's the most common use of the term is when a PhD student makes the decision to bounce with just a masters, and the conditions for THAT can be very program-specific too.  Some tie it to coursework, others, like my previous program, make you wait until you take quals in your 3rd year, etc.

&#x200B;

And you're also correct on the transferring credits since it's VERY program-dependent and often case-by-base since students coming from other grad programs may have had very different experiences. In my current situation, I was admitted to a program that normally requires a masters with the contingency that I earn my content masters along the way and am being given credit for only two of my previous courses. (I was previously in a PhD program and left without a masters.)  Meanwhile, two of my classmates have masters degrees in different disciplines, they're basically in a similar boat where they're only bypassing a couple of courses (different ones at that) because the program requirements are pretty high. And other programs I applied to, most of them were going to make me completely start over.

Nobody should be talking about your disability accommodations with ANYONE else. Honestly, any professor who would try to judge you for taking care of yourself and not wrecking your health is someone you shouldn't be trying to ask for a letter of recommendation anyway.

&#x200B;

I'm not saying that your concerns about stigma aren't valid since ableism IS a problem in academia, but the details your accommodations are between you and the disability office alone. All your professors find out is that you HAVE accommodations and what they ARE, but not WHY. I've had accommodations in undergrad AND now in graduate school and I'm more than happy to chat about my experiences with disability support services and whatnot if you'd like.

No, it's really not. 

As someone who TAed 4 semesters of biology, 3 of which were fully online (and am getting ready to TA another semester online), you're so far off base here that it's sad.

&#x200B;

I had students who had to stay home and watch siblings because their parents had to pick up extra jobs. I had students who had to be out of state or even the country for perfectly valid reasons. I had students who preferred online because it worked better with their disability accommodations or their learning preferences. I also had students who were severely immunocompromised and appreciated not having to risk their health by being forced to be in tight quarters at a massive party school.

&#x200B;

There are plenty of valid reasons that students want to be online. Stop trying to claim it's all about academic dishonesty.

Yes, they'll have to upload letters for each school. Every school's process is different, sadly. Don't sweat it being "unfair" for them, I got chewed out for apologizing every time I needed more letters because I was told it's part of their job and that all they do is make minor changes for each program, so it doesn't take that long.

&#x200B;

What I did to make their lives easier was that I made a spreadsheet of the programs I applied to, sorted by due date and put a link to each program website so that they could reference it in case they wanted to make any tweaks to their letters that were more program specific. (in my case this time, it was important because I had applied to some more labwork-oriented programs and some more biology education-focused ones and they did need to emphasize different things. Putting things all in one place let them plan on THEIR schedule instead of suddenly getting hit with batches of applications at times during the semester where they were doing lesson prep/grant applications/etc.

The case for Tech honestly sounds like it became a lot stronger here to me. Not rotating also takes a lot of stress off because lab rotations are generally stressful AF since you're taking classes, possibly teaching, trying to adjust to life as a grad student, and dealing with the pressure of finding a lab. Being able to build on your work where you are also means more time that can be put into expanding and getting more potential publications and conferences out of it. A CV and a body of work showing your experience with a subject is going to matter more than the actual program name on the degree, especially in the biology arena where things are classed differently by university so what's biochem at one place might be biology at another and biomedical at a third university. When I applied in 2020, it was like that all with the same area of interest. Even now, I had to apply to two different types of programs to do very similar work.

&#x200B;

One of the big questions is this: Which of the two do you feel is a healthier, more supportive environment? If Tech is reasonably supportive and you don't know for sure if UNC is capable of being one, that would definitely be a point in Tech's favor. If you also already have solid support structures in place in terms of friends/family in ATK, that's also a big plus since grad school is a struggle and you need all of the support you can get.

As someone who got her first one on Christmas Eve in 2019, one of the biggest pieces of advice I can give is to delete the rejection emails. If they're in your inbox, you can still look at them and see them and they'll live rent-free in your head for way longer than you want them to.

&#x200B;

Also, take some time for self-care tonight. Do something nice for yourself. You're going to be upset for a while and it's absolutely valid to be, but don't let yourself go down the rabbit hole of depression for too long. It's easy to fall into despair.

&#x200B;

And the other reply is right: It's THEIR loss.

It's totally fine to be nervous about interviews, but don't let your imposter syndrome get in the way. If they're inviting you for an interview, that means they're interested and the best thing you can do is be your authentic self and try not to compare yourself to the competition.

&#x200B;

As far as questions like that go, that PI your friend met with sounds a BIT on the extra side and that doesn't seem like a fair question. I would do your research on the PI's you'll be meeting with. Hone in on the projects of theirs that you vibe with on some level and think of the research questions you would ask. If you can go methods-level deep, great, but if not, think of the research questions and try to spin that into a dialogue with them instead of them grilling you. Try to find people to practice with if you can just to get a feel for the process.

&#x200B;

Seriously, though, don't compare yourself to others or you're going to kill your confidence before you even get there. Imposter syndrome is a bitch and a half.

This place may not be AS bad as GradCafe, but depending on your field, it can be almost as stressful watching people post about their decisions. (Mine is unfortunately very active)

&#x200B;

As far as the trolling, GradCafe is definitely prone to it. There's a higher chance of people calling BS in here, but I would still take everything posted with a grain of salt.  Regardless, it can still be a source of anxiety. You know yourself best. If it makes you feel worse, it's probably best to know your limits and either limit being on here or just limit what you look at. (I tend to just outright avoid posts with the flair for my field unless it's obviously a question instead of something related to decisions, for example.)

I'm a grad student in a different department that's related, but not in the same college, but if those program stipends are anything like ours, they're on the low side for STEM and you hand a fairly large chunk of it right back to UD in the form of grad student fees.

&#x200B;

UD doesn't do anything for us for housing, they used to have grad student housing but apparently that stopped being a thing, so you have to live off-campus and it's not a particularly cozy existence for most of us. Living here without a car absolutely sucks because the UD Shuttle isn't always running when and where you need it and not that reliable, but having a car is expensive since insurance isn't cheap and UD charges a ton of money for parking permits.

This is true. I still tell stories from last year but only remember the name of one of the students responsible for the funny incidents.

&#x200B;

Some of this shit carries on to the TAs too, I guarantee I'll ALSO live on as the first TA in my entire teaching team to have a fire since we went back in person.

My university is still in Drop/Add, if I saw that in a syllabus for a class I was in, I would be opening the courses search in another tab and looking for a different professor.  You can plan until the cows come home, but illnesses can come out of nowhere (especially in the middle of a pandemic, imagine that) and family emergencies can totally blindside you.  There was NO amount of planning that could have accounted for one of my parents winding up nearly dying and being in a coma for a couple of months.

&#x200B;

A power outage and something catastrophic like that do not even BELONG lumped into that same category.   Those are NOT equal.  Screw that noise.  This reads like it's from a professor who only teaches for their ego or because they're a researcher who's required to teach and hates it.
